ratorname{tan}⁡x+1}\\,\\text{d}x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186429930 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "305ab7d8-b9f8-5861-8b01-dd9a503b80df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186429930 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $u=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+1.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}={sec}^{2}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u={sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+1}\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+1}\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}(u-1)\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int (1-\\frac{1}{u})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =u-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+1)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+1|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+1|+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=C+1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186429931 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186429928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186429931 #edb/ref 1069 "q-355818" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186429931 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186429931 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /20}^{\\pi /10}5{csc}^{2}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186429931 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "66f3c3b9-c1e9-58f7-9604-16a02027760f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186429931 #edb/ref 1074 "If we let $u=5x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $\\pi /20$ | $\\pi /10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\pi /4$ | $\\pi /2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /20}^{\\pi /10}5{csc}^{2}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}5{csc}^{2}⁡(u)\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{csc}^{2}⁡(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(u){|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})) \\\\  & =-(0-1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186429932 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186429927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186429932 #edb/ref 1069 "q-10574" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186429932 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186429932 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186429932 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a41efe2e-217f-5d4e-8c40-c317692d2ec6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186429932 #edb/ref 1074 "If we let $u=3x+\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi /9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\pi /3$ | $2\\pi /3$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u{|}_{\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186429933 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186429929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186429933 #edb/ref 1069 "q-10578" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186429933 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186429933 #edb/ref 1071 "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}x=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186429933 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a6be6fa7-2d6e-5c8a-ab1e-bb035341f649" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186429933 #edb/ref 1074 "If we let $u=x-\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives \n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi /2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\pi /3$ | $\\pi /6$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{sec}⁡u\\operatorname{tan}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡u{|}_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /6} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
